<h2>Why the door closes</h2>
<p>Article 136 confers a discretionary, extraordinary jurisdiction: no litigant has a right of appeal under it, only a right to ask. Once the Court has been asked about a particular order and has answered — by refusing leave, dismissing the petition, or permitting its withdrawal — the extraordinary request stands exhausted for that order. Permitting successive petitions against the same order would convert a discretionary gateway into an instrument of attrition, and would sit uneasily with the finality the Court\'s orders are meant to carry. The practice is reinforced by the requirement, in the Supreme Court Rules, that every petition disclose whether an earlier petition against the impugned order was filed, and with what result; suppression of an earlier round is itself treated with severity.</p>

<h2>What each kind of first-round outcome leaves behind</h2>
<table class="law">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Outcome of first SLP</th><th>Fresh SLP against same order?</th><th>What may remain</th></tr>
