    <tr><td>Dismissed for default / non-prosecution</td><td>No fresh SLP; restoration of the same petition is the remedy</td><td>Application to restore, explaining the default</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>The order-wise nature of the bar</h2>
<p>The bar is against re-litigating the same order, not against ever returning to the Supreme Court in the same dispute. Litigation generates orders in sequence — interim orders, final judgments, review orders, orders on remand — and each distinct order is a distinct subject of challenge. A party whose SLP against an interim order failed may still challenge the final judgment when it comes; a party who lost against the main judgment may, in a proper case, challenge a later order passed in review to the extent it decides something new. What a litigant cannot do is dress up the same grievance against the same order in new grounds and present it as a fresh petition.</p>

<h2>Candour is the operating condition</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Every petition under Article 136 must disclose the complete prior history: earlier SLPs, their diary or case numbers, and their outcomes; any withdrawal and the terms recorded; any review pending or decided below. The Supreme Court treats suppression of a previous round as a ground to dismiss outright, with costs in appropriate cases. Where a genuine second trip to the Court is available — because a new order exists, or liberty was granted — the safest course is to put the first round on the face of the petition and explain why the fresh challenge is maintainable.</p>
</div>

<h2>The remedies that actually remain</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><h3>Review below</h3><p>Where the SLP was dismissed in limine, a review petition before the High Court against its own judgment can remain available on review grounds — an error apparent, new and important material, or analogous reasons — within limitation.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Review in the Supreme Court</h3><p>Where the Supreme Court decided the matter, the remedy against that decision is a review petition under Article 137 and the Supreme Court Rules, ordinarily circulated in chambers, within the prescribed period.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Curative jurisdiction</h3><p>After review fails, the curative petition exists for the rarest cases — established violation of natural justice or bias — on a certificate by a senior advocate, and is not a third bite at the merits.</p></div>
  <div class="tile"><h3>Fresh orders, fresh challenge</h3><p>Orders passed after remand, in review, or at subsequent stages generate their own Article 136 window, challenged on their own merits with full disclosure of the history.</p></div>
</div>

<div class="note">
  <p><strong>Practice note:</strong> the most consequential drafting moment in a failing SLP is the withdrawal request — whether liberty to approach the appropriate forum, or to refile on a defined contingency, is sought and recorded. Those few words in the order determine whether any road back exists. This article is for general information only and is not legal advice.</p>
</div>
